  "text": "Question seven. Explain Dijkstra's algorithm and its complexity. It finds shortest paths from one source when no weight is negative, a min-heap handing out the closest unfinished node next. C costs four directly but three through B. D costs four, through B and C. With V nodes and E edges, each node is finished once, and each edge can cost a heap push of log V: V plus E, times log V. Why no negative weights? A node is final when it leaves the heap. B leaves at two, and only later does the minus-two edge from C reach it at one. B is wrong, and so is D. Bellman-Ford instead tries every edge V minus one times: V times E, slower, and right.\n\nQuestion eight. How does quicksort work, and what is its worst case? Pick a pivot, split the rest into smaller and larger, and sort each side. On average, n log n. The worst case is n squared, when the pivot is always the smallest or largest. Pivot a thousand sorted numbers on the last, and each split peels off one: half a million comparisons, nearly a thousand levels. Written recursively, that's past Python's default limit. Random pivots on the same input: eleven thousand comparisons, twenty-one levels. And sorted input is common, rows by ID or events by time, so randomize. Quicksort's edge over merge sort is sorting in place.",
